VALUES('BScN','Paper I','Medical-Surgical Nursing','Respiratory Disorders','What is the primary goal of administering oxygen therapy in nursing practice?','To decrease oxygen levels in the bloodstream','To increase the risk of respiratory distress','To provide supplemental oxygen to patients with respiratory problems','To minimise communication with the patient','C','Target SpO2 94–98% for most patients (88–92% for COPD with CO2 retention). Delivery devices: nasal cannula (1–6 L/min), simple face mask (6–10 L/min), non-rebreather mask (10–15 L/min). BScN nurses titrate O2 to target, not routinely at maximum (Brunner & Suddarth; British Thoracic Society O2 guidelines).','easy',NULL,'approved',NULL)ON CONFLICT(stem,cadre)DO NOTHING;

